## Screen frame container (MANDATORY)

Every delivered screen or flow in Figma MUST live inside a top-level **FRAME**. Do not place DS instances, text, or layout groups as loose children of the page (`figma.currentPage`).

### Rules

1. **One root FRAME per screen** — e.g. `Screen / Create meeting modal`, `1440 — Job overview`. Name clearly; match target width (1440, 1280, 390…).
2. **Everything goes inside** — Modal, Blanket, Sidebar, sections, and all nested instances are **children of that frame**, not siblings on the page root.
3. **Modals** — Screen frame contains Blanket + Modal instance(s). Never leave Modal/Blanket orphaned on the page without a parent frame.
4. **Sub-frames** — Use additional FRAMEs for regions (header, body, footer) with auto-layout when helpful; bind padding, gap, and fills to Phenom variables.
5. **Allowed `createFrame`** — A root or layout frame is required and allowed; the ban on `createFrame` applies only to **substituting DS components** (fake buttons, fake modals), not to the screen container itself.

### Minimal pattern

```js
const screen = figma.createFrame();
screen.name = 'Screen / <screen-name>';
screen.resize(1440, 900);
figma.currentPage.appendChild(screen);

// Append ALL nodes to screen — never directly to figma.currentPage
```

### Definition of done

- [ ] A named root FRAME exists for the screen
- [ ] No orphaned instances or text nodes on the page root
- [ ] Screenshot taken of the frame for review

